> 
> ### `planner/selection.py`
> 
> ```python
> """
> Pure plan-selection policy.
> 
> This module chooses a PlanSpec for a PlannerContext from eligible candidates.
> It applies learned evidence, quality priors, cost, latency, reliability risk,
> availability, and constrained exploration. It never executes plans or mutates
> durable state during selection.
> 
> Conceptual utility:
> 
>     U(p | x) =
>         w_s * predicted_satisfaction
>       + w_q * predicted_quality
>       - w_c * expected_cost
>       - w_l * expected_latency
>       - w_r * expected_risk
>       + w_e * exploration_value
> 
> Quality, reliability, user satisfaction, and cost/latency must remain separate
> evidence streams. Do not allow cheap fluent plans to dominate solely because
> they collect positive thumbs.
> 
> Public API:
> 
> class PlanSelector(Protocol):
>     def select_plan(
>         self,
>         context: PlannerContext,
>         candidate_plans: Sequence[PlanSpec],
>         past_response_records: Sequence[ResponseRecord] | None = None,
>     ) -> PlanSpec: ...
> 
> class BayesSelector:
>     def __init__(
>         self,
>         weights: Mapping[str, float] | None = None,
>         *,
>         random_seed: int | None = None,
>         high_stakes_exploration_limit: float = ...,
>     ): ...
> 
>     def select_plan(...) -> PlanSpec: ...
> 
>     def score_plan(
>         self,
>         plan: PlanSpec,
>         context: PlannerContext,
>         records: Sequence[ResponseRecord],
>     ) -> float: ...
> 
>     def update_weights(self, feedback: Any) -> None:
>         '''
>         Called by evaluation.py or feedback.py. This is not invoked as a hidden
>         side effect of `select_plan`.
>         '''
>         ...
> 
> Caller:
> - planner.py gets candidate plans and invokes `selector.select_plan(...)`.
> - feedback.py/evaluation.py update evidence after outcomes arrive.
> - selection must be deterministic given candidates, context, learned state,
>   and configured/random exploration seed.
> """
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ### `planner/responders.py`
> 
> ```python
> """
> Execution of selected plans.
> 
> Responders take a selected PlanSpec and PlannerContext, construct effective
> messages, invoke registered model adapters and/or tools, and return a Response
> or StreamResponse plus a ResponseRecord.
> 
> Important design rules:
> - Responders execute; they do not choose plans or update durable learning.
> - Tool outputs require provenance/safety handling before model injection.
> - A responder must record model/tool failure, latency, tokens, and estimated
>   or observed cost.
> - For streaming, create the response record before yielding the first token.
> - Before visible output, a failed stream may be retried under planner policy.
>   After visible output, do not silently switch model or plan.
> 
> Public protocol:
> 
> class Responder(Protocol):
>     def chat_response(
>         self,
>         plan: PlanSpec,
>         context: PlannerContext,
>         registry: Registry,
>         *,
>         max_tokens: int | None = None,
>     ) -> tuple[Response, ResponseRecord]: ...
> 
>     def stream_response(
>         self,
>         plan: PlanSpec,
>         context: PlannerContext,
>         registry: Registry,
>         *,
>         max_tokens: int | None = None,
>     ) -> tuple[StreamResponse, ResponseRecord]: ...
> 
> Concrete responders:
> 
> class LLMResponder: ...
> class ToolUseResponder: ...
> class WorkflowResponder: ...
> 
> Caller:
> - planner.py selects the responder appropriate to `plan.template_id` or plan
>   metadata and invokes one of these methods.
> - planner.py persists the resulting ResponseRecord.
> - feedback.py later uses the record for attribution.
> """
> ```
