> 
> ### `planner/feedback.py`
> 
> ```python
> """
> Feedback event capture and response-to-plan attribution.
> 
> Feedback is always linked to immutable response records, not a mutable current
> responder. Thumbs are useful satisfaction signals, but they are not equivalent
> to truthfulness, source quality, or reliability.
> 
> Public API:
> 
> class FeedbackService:
>     def vote_up(self, response_id: str) -> FeedbackEvent: ...
>     def vote_down(self, response_id: str) -> FeedbackEvent: ...
> 
>     def feedback(
>         self,
>         response_id: str,
>         *,
>         kind: Literal["thumb_up", "thumb_down", "correction", "rating", "edit"],
>         value: Any = None,
>     ) -> FeedbackEvent: ...
> 
> Caller:
> - planner.py exposes `vote_up`, `vote_down`, and eventually `feedback`.
> - FeedbackService reads the ResponseRecord from persistence.py.
> - It writes durable feedback events and forwards attributable learning signals
>   to evaluation.py / selection.py.
> """
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ### `planner/metrics.py`
> 
> ```python
> """
> Token, cost, latency, availability, and failure measurement.
> 
> All estimates are explicit and revisable. Observed provider usage should
> eventually supersede static provider price estimates.
> 
> Public helpers:
> 
> def estimate_prompt_tokens(messages: Sequence[Mapping[str, Any]]) -> int: ...
> def estimate_completion_tokens(max_tokens: int | None, plan: PlanSpec) -> int: ...
> def estimate_plan_cost(plan: PlanSpec, prompt_tokens: int, completion_tokens: int) -> float | None: ...
> def measure_latency() -> ContextManager[Callable[[], float]]: ...
> def record_execution_metrics(record: ResponseRecord, ...) -> ResponseRecord: ...
> 
> Caller:
> - selection.py uses estimates as soft cost/latency penalties.
> - responders.py measures observed execution metrics.
> - persistence.py stores metrics.
> - evaluation.py uses failures/reliability statistics.
> """
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ### `planner/persistence.py`
> 
> ```python
> """
> Durable storage interfaces and initial in-memory implementations.
> 
> Persistence is separated from planning so a local prototype can use in-memory
> stores while production uses SQLite, Postgres, Redis, object storage, queues,
> or event infrastructure.
> 
> Persist at minimum:
> - registered model/tool/plan versions;
> - response-to-plan attribution and feedback;
> - quality, reliability, failure, cost, token, and latency evidence;
> - artifacts, exact source excerpts, state patches, and async operations;
> - conversation state, events, evaluation outcomes, and delivery records.
> 
> Public interfaces:
> 
> class PlannerRepository(Protocol):
>     def save_response_record(self, record: ResponseRecord) -> None: ...
>     def get_response_record(self, response_id: str) -> ResponseRecord | None: ...
>     def append_feedback(self, event: FeedbackEvent) -> None: ...
>     def response_records_for_plan(self, plan_id: str, version: str) -> list[ResponseRecord]: ...
>     def append_event(self, event: Event) -> None: ...
>     def events_for_conversation(self, conversation_id: str) -> list[Event]: ...
>     def get_state(self, scope: str, target_id: str) -> Mapping[str, Any]: ...
>     def apply_state_patch(self, patch: StatePatch) -> None: ...
>     def save_artifact(self, artifact: Artifact) -> None: ...
>     def get_artifacts(self, conversation_id: str, ...) -> list[Artifact]: ...
>     def save_operation(self, operation: Operation) -> None: ...
>     def get_operation(self, operation_id: str) -> Operation | None: ...
> 
> class InMemoryRepository(PlannerRepository):
>     '''Thread-safe development implementation.'''
> 
> Caller:
> - planner.py reads/writes response records and state.
> - context.py reads state/artifacts.
> - workflow reducers and workers append events and persist outputs.
> """
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ### `planner/evaluation.py`
> 
> ```python
> """
> Quality, reliability, and offline evaluation.
> 
> This module prevents chat thumbs from becoming the sole optimization target.
> It maintains separate evidence for satisfaction, task quality, factuality,
> source grounding, calibration, policy compliance, tests, and failures.
> 
> Public API:
> 
> class Evaluator(Protocol):
>     def evaluate(
>         self,
>         record: ResponseRecord,
>         response: Response | None,
>         context: PlannerContext,
>     ) -> EvaluationResult: ...
> 
> class EvaluationService:
>     def record_feedback(self, feedback: FeedbackEvent) -> None: ...
>     def record_evaluation(self, result: EvaluationResult) -> None: ...
>     def update_plan_evidence(self, plan: PlanSpec) -> None: ...
>     def run_offline_suite(self, suite: EvaluationSuite) -> list[EvaluationResult]: ...
> 
> Evaluation suites should cover representative real work: research, coding,
> technical/numerical analysis, project continuity, planning, writing, and
> source-grounded factual tasks. Prefer objective tests when possible.
> 
> Caller:
> - feedback.py forwards user feedback.
> - workflow may schedule verification evaluators.
> - selection.py consumes summarized evidence, never raw evaluator internals.
> """
> ```
> 
